 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 27,
   "y": 28,
   "text": "THE SWING BRIDGE. It opens around high tide to let the boats in and out of the upper harbour, and it makes a clatter when it does. The Esk gives the town its fish and, in the right light, its mood."
  },
  {
   "x": 36,
   "y": 28,
   "text": "THE 199 STEPS. Counted by every child who has ever climbed them, and there are 199, unless you count the landing. Coffins were once carried up to the churchyard, with a rest at every landing. The benches are for the living."
  },
  {
   "x": 38,
   "y": 19,
   "text": "STEP COUNT. 100. Halfway. The abbey is still up there. So is the wind."
  },
  {
   "x": 45,
   "y": 13,
   "text": "WHITBY ABBEY. Founded in 657 with St Hilda as abbess. Her cowherd, Caedmon, sang a hymn in English and became the first English poet we know by name. The ruins are from the 13th century, and the wind is older."
  },
  {
   "x": 53,
   "y": 14,
   "text": "DRACULA. In 1890 Bram Stoker stayed in Whitby and was inspired by the abbey, the churchyard and the harbour. In his novel the Count comes ashore from a wrecked ship as a great black dog and races up the steps."
  },
  {
   "x": 13,
   "y": 14,
   "text": "CAPTAIN COOK. James Cook learnt his seamanship on Whitby's coal ships, and his Endeavour was Whitby-built. The whalebone arch nearby remembers the whalers who once sailed from this harbour."
  },
  {
   "x": 26,
   "y": 34,
   "text": "WHITBY HARBOUR. A fishing port, a shipyard and a whaling station in its time. Its fish and chips are the best in the world, according to everybody in Whitby. The seagulls are the most skilled thieves in Yorkshire."
  }
 ],
